00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:05.120
Leah: As we have already discussed coding
standards are essential when working with

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.200
a team, but how does this work in practice?

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.590
A developer team would initially meet to
agree the rules that they would need to

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.779
follow within their coding standards guide.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.380
This could consist of one that already
exists and be used as a template or basis

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.760
for creating their own coding style guide.

00:00:25.509 --> 00:00:31.180
There are style guides and tools for programming
languages, development environments, platforms,

00:00:31.299 --> 00:00:34.240
frameworks, and content management systems.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:36.240
Chris: Style guides

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:41.330
A main consideration for choosing an existing
style guide is the coding languages that are going

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:46.269
to be used, as there are different standards that
are written for different programming languages.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.600
One of the most well used open sourced style guides
for code standards is the Airbnb style guide.

00:00:54.269 --> 00:01:01.110
This includes style guides for JavaScript,
ES5, React, CSS, Sass, and Ruby.

00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:06.480
It can be found on the GitHub platform and
is one of the most popular style guides.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.950
Leah: There are other ‘style guides’, and these include:

00:01:10.959 --> 00:01:10.969
• Codeguide

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:14.169
• Idiomatic

00:01:14.539 --> 00:01:15.470
• Wikimedia

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:16.970
• Google

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.130
• WordPress

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.960
You can check out these websites on our
weblinks section for more information.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:25.740
Chris: Tools

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:33.940
As source code needs to be clean, consistent, and easy to
maintain by existing developers, there are a number of tools

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.860
that can be used in conjunction with the coding standards.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:44.289
For example, StyleLint finds and fixes problems
in code; the tool identifies and reports when the

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.940
code is not consistent with the coding standards.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.800
It is easy to configure as the style guide is
added as the default coding standard and the

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.490
team can also add their own rules if needed.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.659
Leah: Another example is a tool that
formats the code such as Prettier.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:08.170
It is a library with a plugin that can be set to
format your code each time you save your changes.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:15.060
It removes the original styling and makes sure
that all the outputted code is a consistent style.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.089
Other tools that are commonly used include:

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:20.770
• EditorConfig

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:20.790
• JSCS

00:02:24.369 --> 00:02:24.599
• JSHint

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.210
Chris: Official guides and standards

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:34.710
As well as open source style guides there are also
official guides and standards that can be referenced.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:39.379
This can be useful for certain larger
programming languages that are commonly used with

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.539
proprietary software – some examples include:

00:02:42.939 --> 00:02:45.239
• Microsoft .NET Framework

00:02:45.679 --> 00:02:46.779
• Sun Systems

00:02:46.779 --> 00:02:48.910
• Java Programming language

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:51.850
• C++ Programming language

00:02:54.009 --> 00:02:54.750
Leah: Code review

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:01.110
A code review which is also known as a peer
review, is a form of software quality assurance.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:07.540
It is when one or more of the developer team would
check each other’s source code in a given project.

00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:15.859
Typical elements of a code review would be to include
which standards would be used, first passes, using a

00:03:15.859 --> 00:03:22.930
ticketing system, run tests, check any changes made
and then complete the review with an in-depth pass.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:28.649
Code reviews would speed up the process of the
project as this would ensure consistency and that

00:03:28.649 --> 00:03:31.489
the standards are being met across the whole team.

00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:33.349
Chris: Static analysis

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:42.489
Static analysis also known as Static Application Security
Testing [SAST], is a tool that is used to automatically

00:03:42.779 --> 00:03:46.230
check code for weaknesses that may cause issues later on.

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:51.480
It finds potential quality issues in
the code before the program is run.

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:58.439
It would be used to find and fix vulnerabilities,
bugs, code errors, security risks, memory leaks,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:06.039
null pointers, buffer overflow, and any violations of
the coding standard whilst the code is in a ‘static’

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.030
state [meaning whilst the code is not being run].

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.119
It is often used to comply with coding
guidelines and industry standard.

00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:21.029
Typical elements of static analysis would involve,
writing code, running a static code analyser,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.620
reviewing results, fixing any issues and then testing.

00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:29.380
Leah: Examples of static analysis tools include:

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:30.419
• SonarQube

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.470
-
• Checkmarx SAST [CxSAST]

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.350
• Snyk Code, and

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.850
• Veracode Static Analysis.

